Basic Game Rules

This game is played just like it's namesake, the popular board game,
Othello. There is an 8 by 8 playing board and each player has tokens,
one has RED tokens, the other has BLUE tokens. The actual game uses
game pieces which are BLACK on one side and WHITE on the other.
A move consists of placing a single piece on the board with your color
up. The position on the board where this token is placed must
"outflank" one (or more) of your opponent's token(s). To outflank
means to place a token on the board such that your opponent's row
(or rows) of tokens is bordered at each end you a token of your color.
A row may be made up of one or more tokens. All of the opponent's
pieces which are outflanked by the move are then "flipped" over
to show the other color.

With CoCothello, when you make a move outflanking some of the
opponents tokens, they simply change color, as if by magic!

The game starts with each player having two tokens each and they are
arranged on in the center of the playing board. Each player takes
turn at making a play. If you cannot make a play, you MUST forfeit.

The game continues in this manner, with each player taking turns
at placing one token and flipping the captured pieces until one
of three events occur.

1) All squares become occupied

2) either player loses ALL of his/her tokens, or

3) both players are forced (or select) to forfeit their move.

In either case, the score is tallyed and the player with the most
tokens on the board wins. The difference between the two scores is called
the "margin". Naturally, the greater the margin, the better the win.


Installation

The archive file contains the following files:

CoCothello            Game program
cocothello.doc        this file
AIF.oth               MV AIF
icon.cocothello       MV Icon
cocothello.c          C source listing
release.notes         Changes since the last version

All of you OS9 wizards will readily know where each of these files
should reside. For non-OS9 wizards, suggested locations are:

/dd/games/AIF.oth
/dd/cmds/cocothello
/dd/cmds/icons/icon.cocothello

NOTE: This game ONLY runs as a MultiVue application. It opens a type 6
graphics window, which uses 16000 bytes of memory. The program uses another
16K. So, if you have a 128K CoCo 3, you must have at least 40K free to
play this game(doubtful!).

There are some run-time options which may be optionally specified
on the parameter line (line 2) of the AIF. The default monitor type
can be specified with an r (RGB) or a c (CMP). Also, you can specify
type types of play mode. An f means "FAST" mode, which is just like
V1.0 played, but the computer move is still "flashed" prior to effecting
its move. An s means "SLOW" mode. This is just like the way V1.1 played,
that is, after "flashing" its move, the program then waits for a mouse
click before it effects its move.

If no default monitor type is specified, you will be asked what kind of
monitor you are using, as described below. If no default play mode is
specifed, the default is "FAST" mode. Upper or lower case is supported
for ALL options. If both monitor type and play mode options are specified
do NOT put a space between them.

MultiVue is not needed if you run this program via Jim Smith's
excellant utility, TYPE. It is available on all major information
systems. Runtime options may be specified after the pathname, ie,

OS9: type 6 cocothello rf

I have tested this, and it works just fine. Nice job, Jim!

In the following instructions, "pointing" to an object means to move
the mouse pointer and "clicking" means to press the mouse button
(or if you are using a joystick, press the "Fire" button).

Since this game runs under OS9 and uses the "autofollow" mouse, the
keyboard mouse is also supported. If you don't have a mouse or joystick,
press CTRL/CLEAR to switch over to the keyboard mouse. For more
information on the use of the keyboard mouse, check page 2-2 of the
Multi-Vue manual.

To crank up the game, you must select the directory which contains the
AIF. When you do, the game icon will be displayed. Double click on the
game icon. If you did not specify a default monitor type as runtime
parameter, you are first presented with a screen used to determine your
monitor type. Just click on RGB or CMP, depending on which type of
monitor you are using. You then will see an introductory banner screen.
To begin play, click the mouse.

The initial playing board is then displayed. It is on the left side of the
screen. When it is your move, to select a square to occupy, simply
point the arrow cursor to the square desired and click the mouse.

You are first asked three questions. You are given choices for each
question. To select the choice, point to it and click the mouse.
The first question is whether you want the game to play it's best
strategy ot not. What this means is that if you select YES, during the
play of the next game, the computer will tend to select the corners
if it is one of the computer's valid moves.

The next question is which color you want to play and the last question
whether you want to go first ot not. If you click on NO, the computer
goes ahead and moves before allowing you to.

After you make a play, the computer will tell you how many of it's
pieces you have captured and update the score. It will then make
it's move. When it has decided, it will "flash" the square it has
selected with one of it's game pieces. If you are in "SLOW" mode,
it will then wait for a mouse click before it shows you your pieces
that it has captured and updates the score. In "FAST" mode, no mouse
click is required.

The right side has the current score. The right side is also
used for informational messages, such as "invalid move" and "square is
already occupied".

Also, in the right area are the three words, QUIT, NEW GAME, and FORFEIT.
These three special commands are more or less self-explanatory. Clicking
on them causes that function to be performed. If you need to forfeit
your move (yes it CAN happen in CoCothello!), click on the word FORFEIT.

At any time, you may give up on the current game and have a new game
started by clicking on "NEW GAME". When you have finished playing and
want the program to exit, click on "QUIT".


I developed CoCothello on a CoCo 3 with 512K, OS9 LII, OS9 C
Compiler, C Graphics Library, & Multi-Vue. This program also depends
on the supplemental clib.l library written by Carl Kreider. My system
also has a Seagate ST225 20Mb hard disk with the Burke & Burke CoCoXT-RTC
interface, two 3.5" 720Kb floppies, and one 5.25" 360Kb floppy.
I also have a Tandy CM-8 RGB monitor, for which this game was
specifically designed. I did take a look at it on a TV and it
didn't look too bad. If you do use a TV, be sure to select the CMP
palette.


